Kernir patch 0017: the xar reader joins the pieces of a <name> or <link> text

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_xar.c (struct xar, xml_start, xml_data).
Reported upstream as https://github.com/libarchive/libarchive/issues/3849. Applies on top of 0001-0016 (0015 and
0016 lines included).

With expat (the only XML parser in this build), the CharacterDataHandler hands over the text of one element in
pieces: at every entity or character reference (&amp; &lt; &gt; &#233;), at each newline, and where the 64 KiB
chunk of inflated TOC that expat_read_toc feeds to XML_Parse ends. xml_data kept only the first piece of a <name>
(the HAS_PATHNAME guard added upstream for repeated <name> elements, #2151/#2236, also dropped the rest of the
first one) and only the last piece of a <link> (archive_strncpy). So "Tom &amp; Jerry.txt" and "Tom &amp;
Mary.txt" both read as "Tom " and extract over each other, a name across a chunk boundary is cut there, and
"a&amp;b" links to "b". Nothing is reported.

xml_start now records, when a <name> starts, whether the entry already has a pathname (kernir_name_ignored: only
the first <name> counts, as upstream intends), and empties the symlink target when a <link> starts (the last <link>
counts, whole), uncharging what it held from 0015's names total. xml_data adds the parent prefix with the first
piece of a name and appends every piece of a name or link target. 0015's per-name and per-link limit (65,536
bytes, parent prefix included) and 0016's names total are charged on the joined text, by what each piece adds, so
a name cannot pass the limits by arriving in pieces. Names with enctype="base64" are still decoded per piece by
strappend_base64: a name wrapped into lines of whole 4-character groups now decodes whole, but one split at a chunk
boundary inside a group still decodes wrongly (the issue's open point; decoding in xml_end would fix it). Archives whose names come in one piece read as before.

One behaviour differs from upstream: "<link>a</link><link/>" now leaves an empty target (upstream kept "a") and
HAS_SYMLINK stays set. This is harmless: read_header copies only a non-empty target into the entry.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0017-xar-name-chardata.patch

--- a/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_xar.c
